  +# Self-Update Feature Summary
  +
  +## Original Requirements
  +
  +From `README.md`, the original requirements were:
  +
  +1. **Explicit commands:**
  +   - `cpe update` — resolve latest GitHub release, compare against baked-in `CPE_VERSION`, download matching asset, verify SHA256 checksum, atomically replace the running binary, and report `vOLD → vNEW`
  +   - `cpe update --check` — report current vs latest version; change nothing
  +
  +2. **Automatic updating:**
  +   - `cpe start` (long-running TUI): check every 6 hours in background and auto-install when newer release exists. After successful install, show persistent yellow footer badge — `↑ vX.Y.Z installed — restart to apply` — in all TUI modes
  +   - Short-lived CLI commands: throttled check (at most once per 24 h, cached in `~/.local/state/cpe/update-check.json`). When newer version exists, print one stderr line: `update available: vX.Y.Z — run cpe update`. Short-lived commands never install.
  +
  +3. **Configuration and guards:**
  +   - `update_auto?: boolean` in `AppConfig` (`~/.config/cpe/config.json`), default `true`. Setting `false` disables both TUI auto-update loop and CLI nudge. Explicit `cpe update` always works.
  +   - Dev builds (`CPE_VERSION === 'dev'`) never check, nudge, or auto-install.
  +
  +4. **Release integrity:**
  +   - Release workflow publishes `SHA256SUMS` file alongside binaries
  +   - Updater downloads `SHA256SUMS` for target tag and verifies binary before swapping
  +   - Fails closed (clear error, no swap) on mismatch or missing file
  +   - `scripts/install.sh` also verifies when `SHA256SUMS` is present
  +
  +## What Was Built
  +
  +### Phase 01 — Release checksums (SHA256SUMS)
  +**Status:** Complete
  +
  +- Added `SHA256SUMS` file to every GitHub release containing checksums for all binaries using bare asset names
  +- Modified `scripts/install.sh` to download and verify checksums when available, falling back gracefully to older releases that don't include the checksums file
  +- Updated `docs/install-and-release.md` to document this change
  +
  +### Phase 02 — Updater core module (src/update/)
  +**Status:** Complete
  +
  +- Created `src/update/` directory with core update engine:
  +  - `check.ts` - Version resolution and comparison functions (`fetchLatestTag`, `compareVersions`, `isNewer`)
  +  - `install.ts` - Verified atomic binary installation (`assetName`, `downloadAndInstall`)
  +  - `state.ts` - Persisted check-state (`readUpdateState`, `writeUpdateState`, `shouldCheck`)
  +- Added comprehensive tests for all modules
  +- All modules are unit-testable without network access
  +
  +### Phase 03 — CLI surface: `cpe update`, config flag, nudge
  +**Status:** Complete
  +
  +- Added `cpe update` command with `--check` option
  +- Added `update_auto` config flag to `AppConfig` in `src/types/meta.ts`
  +- Implemented post-command update nudge for short-lived commands
  +- Updated `README.md` with documentation of the update feature
  +- Modified `src/index.ts` to use `await program.parseAsync()` and call `maybePrintUpdateNudge()`
  +- Added command registration in `src/cli.ts`
  +
  +### Phase 04 — TUI auto-update loop + footer badge
  +**Status:** Complete
  +
  +- Implemented background auto-update loop in `src/update/auto.ts`
  +- Added persistent yellow footer badge in all TUI modes (`Watch`, `Manage`, `Bench`)
  +- Created `src/tui/hooks/useInstalledUpdate.ts` hook for React state management
  +- Updated `docs/DESIGN.md` with badge specification (placement, color, copy, persistence)
  +- Integrated the auto-update loop with `src/commands/start.ts`
  +
  +## Lessons Learned
  +
  +1. **Architecture:** The modular approach with separate check, install, and state modules made testing and maintenance straightforward.
  +
  +2. **Version Comparison:** The `compareVersions` function needed to handle complex cases like prerelease versions (`v1.0.0-rc.1` vs `v1.0.0`) and missing segments (1.0 vs 1.0.0).
  +
  +3. **File Handling:** Using temporary files and atomic `rename()` operations for binary replacement was crucial for avoiding `ETXTBSY` errors on Linux/macOS.
  +
  +4. **Error Handling:** All failure paths in the update process properly clean up temporary files and preserve the original binary, ensuring robustness.
  +
  +5. **User Experience:** The persistent footer badge in TUI modes effectively communicates the need to restart sessions without being intrusive.
  +
  +6. **Throttling:** The 24-hour throttle for CLI nudge and 6-hour interval for auto-update were well-chosen to balance user needs with resource usage.
  +
  +7. **Testing:** The ability to mock network operations and file system access was crucial for comprehensive testing of the update module.
  +
  +8. **Documentation:** The integration of design documentation in `docs/DESIGN.md` was helpful for maintaining consistent TUI behavior.
  +
  +9. **Edge Cases:** The handling of dev builds, empty config files, and network failures was important for a production-ready solution.
  +
  +10. **Security:** The checksum verification approach ensures that only authentic binaries are installed, preventing supply chain attacks.
  +
  +The implementation successfully fulfills all the original requirements with a robust, well-tested, and user-friendly solution.
